# The Larkdowns around Larkfield: sheep on the high folds, drovers, shepherds' pipes and tally sticks, the wind, the
# old barrows and the chalk wolf, and the town's own customs (the Singing Lark's lamp, the chapel bell, the granary,
# harvest home, the frost fair). Downs-wide scenes use the downs group; the carters, the road and the market's
# trade are in larkfield.scene.

scene rg_wm_days_walk
when topic=smalltalk|lore here.region=downs
a : Ridgemere's days and days north of here. More with sheep. | Only one city, and it's far enough off to suit me. | Nobody out here measures in miles. It's days' walk or nothing.
b[herder] : Twice that with my sheep. Stubborn beasts.
b[old] : I walked to the city once. Came back and never left again.
b : Far enough. | Suits me too. | Miles are for cities.
a : Far enough. | Good. | Cities can keep them.

scene rg_wm_lambing
when topic=work|lore here.region=downs here.season=spring
a : Lambing's started on the high fold. Nobody's sleeping. | Twin lambs at dawn, both alive. | Lost a ewe in the night. Lamb lived, though.
b[herder] : Never trust a wolf who hasn't sat up a lambing night.
b[farmer] : Spring's the hardest and the best, both at once.
b : Busy season. | Good start. | Small mercies.
a : The busiest. | Very good. | Small ones.

scene rg_wm_lantern_window
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield here.time=dusk|night
a : Every house on the road keeps a lantern in the window. | The Singing Lark's lamp's lit every night for travellers. Has been for a hundred years. | Put the lantern out for your son tonight.
b[old] : A lantern in the window means somebody's still walking home.
b[innkeeper] : That lamp's never gone out. Not once. My grandmother's oath.
b : An old custom. | A hundred years. | I do, every night.
a : A good one. | At least. | I know.

scene rg_wm_barrows_luck
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : Touched the old barrow stone before the market. For luck. | Somebody dug at the north barrow. Bad business. | The barrows are older than any town on the downs.
b[old] : Touch the stone, don't take from it. That's the rule.
b[priest] : The barrows are the old dead. Leave them in peace.
b : Did it work? | Very bad. | Older than the city.
a : We'll see. | Very bad. | Much older.

scene rg_wm_mercantile_ledger
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : My name's on three pages of the general store's ledger. | The store's ledger is thicker than a chapel book. | They say the ledger's got debts from my grandfather in it still.
b[merchant] : Every debt's honest, and every debt gets paid. Eventually.
b : Mine's on four. | Everybody's in it. | Probably true.
a : Four! | Everybody. | Probably.

scene rg_wm_drovers
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=herder|carter|merchant
a : Drovers in off the downs with a hundred head. | Cattle prices fell at the last market. | A drover lost twenty head to a storm on the downs.
b[herder] : Drive slow, drive steady, and never cross the barrows at night.
b : Good market, then. | Bad for us. | Bad luck.
a : Very good. | Very bad. | Very bad luck.

scene rg_wm_hide_yard
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : Wind's from the tanner's yard today. Shut the shutters. | The tanner's soaking a new batch. Whole town knows it. | Nobody buys a house downwind of the tanner.
b[crafter] : Leather's leather. The smell pays for the bread.
b[child] : It smells like a dead thing! Yuck!
b : Already did. | Whole town. | Nobody sane.
a : Good. | Everybody. | Nobody.

scene rg_wm_down_chapel_bell
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : The chapel bell carries for miles. Shepherds hear it on the high fold. | Ring the bell three times for a lost traveller, they say. | The bell rang at midnight last week. Nobody knows who.
b[priest] : The bell was cast to carry. That's its whole purpose out here.
b[herder] : Heard it from two days out once. Felt like home calling.
b : For miles. | So they say. | Strange.
a : For miles. | Old custom. | Very strange.

scene rg_wm_watch_map
when topic=lore|work here.region=larkfield
a : The map on the guardroom wall's covered in pins. Every one a farm. | The watch rode out to the far farms again. | Half the pins on that map have no one left on them.
b[guard] : We ride the circuit every week. Every farm.
b : That's a lot of pins. | Hard ride. | Emptier every year.
a : A lot. | Very hard. | Every year.

scene rg_wm_emptying_downs
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield pair.band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : The downs get emptier every year. Nobody knows why. | Three farms left empty since spring. | Young ones go north to the city and never come back.
b[old] : When I was young you could see a farm smoke from every hill.
b[adolescent] : There's nothing out here but sheep and wind.
b : Hard to say. | Sad to see. | Can you blame them?
a : Hard. | Very sad. | Sometimes.

scene rg_wm_granary
when topic=work|lore here.region=larkfield
a : The granary's half full. Hard winter coming. | They count the granary sacks every week now. | The granary's the only stone building in town that doesn't leak.
b[farmer] : Fill it in autumn, live through winter. Simple.
b[official] : Every sack counted, every sack recorded.
b : Let's hope not. | Wise. | Good thing too.
a : Let's hope. | Very wise. | Very good.

scene rg_wm_marrows_herbs
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : The herbalist sold me a charm for my bad knee. | The herbalist knows every plant on the downs by smell. | Old Marrow's out gathering again. Shop's shut.
b[healer] : Charms help the ones who believe. Herbs help everyone.
b : Did it work? | Every one. | Again.
a : A little. | Every one. | Again.

scene rg_wm_frontier_pride
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : Nobody tells Larkfield what to do. Not the city, not anyone. | We grow our own and kneel to no House. | A city clerk comes down every few years with a ledger. He never stays.
b[old] : That's the downs. We look after ourselves.
b[official] : Somebody still has to keep the roads.
b : Free as the wind. | And proud of it. | Who would?
a : Free. | Very proud. | Nobody.

scene rg_wm_drovers_block
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=street|market|work
a : The mutton stall has a whole carcass hung by the awning. | Best mutton on the downs, at the market. | The butcher's hooks are empty. Drovers are late.
b[cook] : Mutton stew with downland herbs. Nothing better.
b : Good timing. | So they say. | Again.
a : Very good. | Best anywhere. | Again.

scene rg_wm_letter_east
when topic=smalltalk here.region=downs pair.band=friends|family|spouses
a : Still no letter from my son in the city. | A carter brought a letter for the Thornwell widow. First in a year. | Letters take a season to reach the downs.
b[old] : No news means no bad news. That's what I tell myself.
b : It'll come. | Good for her. | If they come.
a : I hope. | Very good. | If.

scene rg_wm_sheep_wander
when topic=work here.region=downs a.job=herder|farmer
a : Half my flock wandered over the ridge in the night. | The sheep are restless. Something's out on the downs. | Sheared forty this week. My back's a hoop.
b[herder] : Sheep go where they please. You just follow and swear.
b : Find them? | Bad sign. | Rest it.
a : Most. | Very bad. | Can't.

scene rg_wm_last_lantern
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=tavern
a : Long tables full tonight. Drovers and traders both. | The Singing Lark's floor is more mud than boards on market night. | Somebody's telling the barrow ghost story again.
b[innkeeper] : Full tables, full till. I'm not complaining.
b : Busy night. | Every market. | Every night.
a : Very busy. | Every one. | Every night.

scene rg_wm_buried
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : They say something's buried under the chapel floor. | My grandmother swore a chest lies under the chapel altar. | Nobody digs under chapels. Not out here.
b[priest] : Under the chapel floor is the chapel's business.
b[old] : I know what's there. I'll take it to the barrows with me.
b : Treasure? | A chest of what? | Nobody sane.
a : Who knows? | She never said. | Nobody.

scene rg_wm_chalk_figures
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : The great chalk wolf on the hillside needs scouring again. | Children climbed the chalk wolf's tail. Rubbed a stripe off it. | Nobody knows who cut the chalk wolf.
b[old] : Scour it every seven years or it fades and the luck goes.
b[child] : We were only playing!
b : Seven years already? | Little pests. | Older than the barrows.
a : Already. | Little pests. | Probably.

scene rg_wm_frontier_market
when topic=work here.region=larkfield here.place=market|street
a : Traders from four towns at market today. | The city traders sell cloth, we sell wool. Round it goes. | A trader from Southmeadow brought grain at a fair price.
b[merchant] : Larkfield market's the only one worth the walk, this side of the city.
b : Busy market. | Round and round. | Rare thing.
a : Very busy. | Round and round. | Very rare.

scene rg_wm_wind_downs
when topic=weather|smalltalk here.region=downs here.weather=clear|overcast|storm
a : Wind off the downs could flay a wolf. | You can see weather coming a day off on the downs. | Wind's dropped. First still day in a month.
b[herder] : Wind's the only thing out here that never leaves.
b : Always is. | That's the downs. | Enjoy it.
a : Always. | It is. | I will.

scene rg_wm_debt_accord
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield pair.band=friends|acquaintances
a : Half of Larkfield came here to get away from something. | Nobody asks where you came from, out here. | My neighbour came south to dodge a debt in the city.
b[old] : Out here, you are what you do. Not what you did.
b : Most of us. | Good rule. | Smart neighbour.
a : Most. | Very good. | Smart.

scene rg_wm_downs_smithy
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=smith|apprentice|farmer
a : Ploughshares at the smithy, three weeks' wait. | The farrier's corner is full of drovers' horses. | Bent my share on a barrow stone.
b[smith] : Barrow stones break everything. Plough round them.
b : Busy smith. | Always is. | Bad luck.
a : Very busy. | Always. | Very bad.

scene rg_wm_shepherd_pipe
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=downs
a : You can hear the shepherds' pipes on the high fold at dusk. | Old shepherds play the same tune every night. Nobody knows its name. | My father piped to his flock. They came running.
b[herder] : The sheep know the tune. That's all it's for.
b : Lovely sound. | An old one. | Clever father.
a : Very lovely. | Very old. | Very clever.

scene rg_wm_far_fire
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield here.time=night
a : Saw a fire on the far downs last night. No farm out there. | Lights moving between the barrows. | Somebody's camping on the old road.
b[guard] : We'll ride out at dawn. Probably drovers.
b[old] : Don't go near lights between the barrows. Never.
b : Drovers. | Fireflies. | Travellers.
a : Probably.

scene rg_wm_harvest_home
when topic=lore|work here.region=larkfield here.season=autumn
a : Last sheaf's in. Harvest home tonight. | The whole town helps bring in the far fields. | They hang the last sheaf in the chapel till spring.
b[farmer] : Every wolf with paws helps at harvest. Even the watch.
b[priest] : The last sheaf is the Bright One's share.
b : Good harvest? | That's Larkfield. | Old custom.
a : Good enough. | It is. | Very old.

scene rg_wm_wolves_wild
when topic=lore here.region=downs
a : Wild dogs took two lambs off the high fold. | Something big has been at the sheep on the east down. | The old shepherds say the downs have teeth.
b[herder] : Wild dogs, nothing more. But I'll sleep by the fold tonight.
b : Bad luck. | Dogs, probably. | They do.
a : Very bad. | Probably. | They do.

scene rg_wm_never_city
when topic=smalltalk here.region=downs a.stage=old
a : Never seen the city. Never wanted to. | Folk in the city live in each other's laps. | My whole life on these downs and I'd do it again.
b[adolescent] : I'd like to see it. Just once.
b : Lucky you. | Too close. | Good life.
a : Lucky. | Far too close. | A good one.

scene rg_wm_tally_sticks
when topic=lore|work here.region=downs
a : Shepherds still keep count on tally sticks. | Old shepherds count sheep in the old numbers. Yan, tan, tethera. | Somebody's tally stick says forty. The fold says thirty-nine.
b[herder] : Tally sticks don't lie. Shepherds do.
b : Old ways. | Older than the town. | One's on the downs.
a : The best ways. | Much older. | Probably.

scene rg_wm_frost_fair
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield here.season=winter
a : The frost fair's on the green next week. | They roast a whole sheep at the frost fair. | The frost fair's the only time the downs folk all come in.
b[innkeeper] : Every bed in the Singing Lark's booked for the frost fair.
b : Can't wait. | The smell! | Once a year.
a : Nor me. | Wonderful. | Once.
